Hello All,

Harco's position on use of Harco Joint Restraints on PE pipe is that stiffe=
ners must be used.

Following is an elaboration of the matter.

Discussions with other joint restraint manufacturers reveal that they have =
had failures when stiffeners are not used. All credible joint restraint man=
ufacturers require the use of stiffeners.

We have done some timed testing on 2" DR 11 pipe that hadn't failed up to t=
he point the test was discontinued but we have no method or criteria to mak=
e any judgment.  Even if we could, we have no basis to extrapolate to all s=
izes and DR's.  Even with a method we would be faced with testing each size=
 against multiple DR's.  It could also be resin specific.  Duraline for ins=
tance uses six different 4710 resins alone.  I am aware that restraints hav=
e been used without stiffeners but we cannot know if there have been no fai=
lures and even if there haven't been any  failures "tomorrow is another day=
".  The failure mechanisms are time dependent.  There are some famous failu=
res on gas lines causing much property damage and loss of life in the Unite=
d States that resulted in Federal regulations requiring stiffeners to be us=
ed.

Leemco's former position that stiffeners are not required has caused them p=
roblems.  They now offer stiffeners.  In the case of their lateral valves t=
hey have a recommendation for stiffeners depending on DR.  Keep in mind tha=
t we have a hyper engineered stiffener for our LIV's that is easy to instal=
l and very low cost minimizing this hypothetical disadvantage.

I am aware that Philmac doesn't require stiffeners.  We have challenged the=
m to provide us the story that explains this.  Anyway, it would be impruden=
t to extrapolate their experience to our product and to larger diameters.

Generally there is currently no great advantage to be able to warrant this =
capability.  We must be wary of allowing our burden of proof to migrate fro=
m "knowing it will work" to "hoping it will work".  Or to use a process tha=
t presumes something is good until it is proven to be no good.

This is an industry convention we have no great commercial incentive to dep=
art from at this time.  Because this commercial incentive is small it is no=
t a high priority to develop the qualification methods and products to achi=
eve a "no stiffener" offering.

If at some time we can accomplish a "no stiffener" requirement then we will=
 change our recommendation.

John


